title: Acute Renal Infarction
---

# Acute Renal Infarction

- **Definition** - uncommon condition occuring as a result of sudden occlusion of the renal arteries
- **Etiology of acute renal infarction:**
  - <u>Thromboembolism</u> - from a distant source
  - <u>Local atherosclerosis</u> - acute plaque changes within the renal artery resulting in atheroembolic complications
- **Clinical features of acute renal infarction:**
  - <u>Loin pain</u> - acute onset of loin pain over the affected side
  - <u>Haematuria</u> - microscopic haematuria that is detected on urine dipstick and urine microscopy
  - <u>Severe hypertension</u> - common, but not universal, presumably as a result of residual renal perfusion is required to generate renin release
  - <u>Acute jidney injury and anuria</u> - only occurs if there is occlusion of bilateral main renal arteries, or occlusion of a single functioning kidney
- **Signs:**
  - May result signs of aortic disease (e.g. weak femoral pulses)
- **Ix:**
  - **Bloods** - signs of ischaemia and tissue damage:
    - <u>Lactate</u> - elevated
      - <u>LDH</u> - elevated
    - <u>CRP</u> - elevated
    - <u>RFT</u> - derranged if causing AKI
  - **CT abdomen** - showing infarcts within renal parenchyma
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Anticoagulation</u> - if source of thromboembolism is identified
    - <u>Angioplasty and stenting</u> - if caused by occlusion of the main renal vessel
